Washington, D.C. – U.S. Rep. Allyson Schwartz (PA-13) issued the following statement today after the White House Council of Economic Advisors released a new report entitled “Missed Opportunities: The Consequences of State Decisions Not to Expand Medicaid.” The report confirms that Governor Tom Corbett’s refusal to expand Medicaid is damaging Pennsylvania by costing the Commonwealth thousands of jobs and preventing hundreds of thousands of people from gaining access to health coverage. 
 “Enough is enough. Governor Tom Corbett’s chosen path of politically motivated delays and inaction is hurting Pennsylvania’s families and economy,” said Congresswoman Allyson Schwartz. “It’s time to expand Medicaid so that we can grow our economy and provide hundreds of thousands of Pennsylvanians with access to affordable coverage.”

According to the report, in Pennsylvania, expansion would:
 Increase the number of people with access to affordable health insurance by 305,000 by 2016;
Create over 35,000 jobs from 2014 to 2017;
Provide an $8.2 billion net increase in federal dollars to the Commonwealth from 2014 to 2016;
Result in 11,100 more people receiving mammograms annually;
Mean 17,600 additional people would receive pap smears annually; and
Reduce the number of people with catastrophic out-of-pocket costs by 13,700 in a typical year.
 In June 2013, Schwartz led the Pennsylvania Democratic Congressional delegation in urging Governor Corbett to move forward without further delay to expand Medicaid coverage. Her letter was signed by all Democratic House members of Pennsylvania’s Congressional delegation: Reps. Matthew A. Cartwright, Robert A. Brady, Michael F. Doyle and Chaka Fattah.
The full report can be found at http://www.whitehouse.gov/expanding-medicaid.
 
